cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use warpdom, please cite it using the metadata below.
type: software
title: "warpdom: a fast, jsdom-compatible DOM for unit and component tests"
version: 0.1.0
# DOI: assigned by Zenodo at the v0.1.0 release; add it here afterwards.
# doi: "10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX"
# date-released: "YYYY-MM-DD"  (the v0.1.0 release date)
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/sonukapoor/warpdom"
url: "https://github.com/sonukapoor/warpdom"
authors:
  - family-names: Kapoor
    given-names: Sonu
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-6074-6238"
abstract: >-
  warpdom is a DOM implementation for JavaScript unit and component tests,
  written from the WHATWG and CSSOM specifications, that installs as a
  drop-in Vitest or Jest environment in place of jsdom. On the open-source
  test suites measured, tests keep the results they have under jsdom apart
  from a short, documented list of differences (tests that fail or are
  flaky on jsdom, deliberate deviations, and order- or timing-dependent
  tests), and suites run faster drop-in (median about 1.3x), with
  larger gains where per-file environment isolation dominates run time.
keywords:
  - DOM
  - jsdom
  - testing
  - unit testing
  - Vitest
  - Jest
  - test environment
  - web platform
  - WHATWG
  - Web Platform Tests
references:
  - type: software
    title: "dom-api-tracer: measure which DOM APIs real test suites use"
    authors:
      - family-names: Kapoor
        given-names: Sonu
    repository-code: "https://github.com/sonukapoor/dom-api-tracer"
    doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23378610"
    notes: >-
      The measurement tool and research harness used to evaluate warpdom.
# preferred-citation: add the accompanying article (type: article, title,
# authors, journal, year, volume, issue, start, end, doi) when it is
# published. Until then GitHub's "Cite this repository" cites the software.
